{:edb/db-before-t 5765 :edb/db-after-t 5766 :edb/tx-data [[13194139539078 50 #inst "2026-10-23T17:52:40.421Z" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186338907 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186338907 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186338908 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-8{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186338908 1071 "Calculate $\\int (-8{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186338916 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186338916 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}z.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186338916 1074 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {z}^{0}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{z}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}z+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186338916 1074 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {z}^{0}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{z}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}z+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186338945 1071 "Simplify the expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{6}}.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186338945 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{6}}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186338947 1071 "Simplify the expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )]}^{6}}.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186338947 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )]}^{6}}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186338965 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4,2,1) & ={4}^{2}+3(2-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=18.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+3(y-1)^{2}-{z}^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(4,2,1)$ is the hyperboloid of one sheet $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18}=1.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186338965 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4,2,1) & ={4}^{2}+3(2-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=18.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+3(y-1)^{2}-{z}^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(4,2,1)$ is the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18}=