e interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\n- The second set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,4).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,2).$\n\n- The third is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0<{x}^{2}<9,\\\\\\,1<y\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186338998 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\n- The second set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,4).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,2).$\n\n- The third is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0<{x}^{2}<9,\\\\\\,1<y\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186339004 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet  $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{5}=1,\n$$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186339004 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet  $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{5}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186339009 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186339009 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186339011 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int -5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186339011 1071 "$\\int -5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186339013 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {\\pi }^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186339013 1071 "$\\int {\\pi }^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186339020 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=25$ and the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t+5 \\\\ y &=-2t, \\\\ z &=2\\sqrt{3}t\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186339020 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=25$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t+5 \\\\ y &=-2t, \\\\ z &=2\\sqrt{3}t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186339024 1071 "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the hyperboloid of one sheet $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6}=1?\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186339024 1071 "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6}=1?$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186339026 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $y.$ First, we solve for $y$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-y & =2\\,⟹\\,y=z-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $y$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(z-2)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({z}^{2}-4z+4)+{z}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{z}^{2}-4z+4 & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{z}^{2}-4z+4 & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{z}^{2}-4z+2 & =6 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2({z}^{2}-2z+1) & =6 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2(z-1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{3}=1.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186339026 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $y.$ First, we solve for $y$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-y & =2\\,⟹\\,y=z-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $y$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(z-2)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({z}^{2}-4z+4)+{z}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{z}^{2}-4z+4 & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{z}^{2}-4z+4 & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{z}^{2}-4z+2 & =6 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2({z}^{2}-2z+1) & =6 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2(z-1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{3}=1.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186339027 1071 "What is the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{z}^{2}=1\n$$ and the plane $z=1$ onto the $xy$-plane?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186339027 1071 "What is the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{z}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=1$ onto the $xy$-plane?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186339032 1071 "Find the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=-1\n$$ and the plane $y-2x=2$ onto the $xz$-plane." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186339032 1071 "Find the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=-1$ and the plane $y-2x=2$ onto the $xz$-plane." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186339032 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $y.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\ny-2x=2\\,⟹\\,y=2(x+1)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the hyperboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2(x+1))^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-{x}^{2}-2x-1+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2x-1+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2x+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{({x}^{2}+4x+4)-4}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}+2+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the hyperbola $$\n\\