 =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is false. The integral in statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1-1=0.\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is convergent” is true." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186351298 1074 "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent” is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\